title: Eligibility
tags: [[File tracking]]
kind: reference
summary: The algorithm deciding whether a path is tracked — nearest mf_watch, a direct watch overriding ignores, patterns matched from the tracking scope — and how to ask it.

For a repository-relative path `p` whose own metarecord is `e`:

# Find ''watch_ancestor'': the nearest metarecord on the chain from `e` up (itself included) that has `mf_watch`. None → ''skip'' (the opt-in default).
# Its `mf_watch` is `false` → ''skip''.
# It is `e` itself — `mf_watch` set ''directly'' — → ''track'', whatever the patterns.
# Find ''ignore_ancestor'': the nearest ancestor of `e` (itself excluded) with at least one `mf_ignore` row.
# Re-anchor `p` at watch_ancestor (remove its directory prefix; unchanged when it is the root). If a pattern of ignore_ancestor matches → ''skip''.
# Otherwise → ''track''.

Step 3 is the per-metarecord override, and step 5 makes a directly watched directory a
new ''tracking scope'': its descendants still go through the patterns, but matched from
that directory down. So watching a directory whose own name an ancestor ignores — a
hidden `.config`, `.git` itself — tracks its contents, while `.git`, `node_modules`,
hidden entries ''below'' it stay ignored. To give it different patterns, set
`mf_ignore` on it.

A skipped directory skips its whole subtree without evaluating it. The watcher and
reconcile both apply this before creating, updating or clearing anything; an ineligible
path is silently left alone.

!! Asking

`POST /repos/:repo/eligibility` runs it for up to 1000 repository-relative paths
(leading `/`, `""` the root), with the reason — what the [[file manager|file-manager panel]] marks rows with:

```
{"paths": ["/music/track.flac", "/target/debug/deps"]}
→ {"results": [
    {"path": "/music/track.flac", "eligible": true, "reason": "tracked", "watch_scope": "", "ignore_source": "", "pattern": null},
    {"path": "/target/debug/deps", "eligible": false, "reason": "ignored", "watch_scope": "", "ignore_source": "", "pattern": "(^|/)target/…"}]}
```

`reason` is the step that decided — `no_watch`, `watch_false`, `direct_watch`,
`ignored`, `tracked`; `watch_scope` the tracking scope's path (`null` without any
`mf_watch`); `ignore_source` the directory whose set applied (`null` if none);
`pattern` the one that matched. Whether a change at a path would ''actually'' be
recorded right now — the watcher's live coverage included — is `mf watch check`'s
question, not this one.

`GET /repos/:repo/ignore/effective?path=<dir>` answers which set ''governs writes''
at a directory: `{"source": "/music", "source_uuid": "…", "direct": false, "patterns":
[…]}` — `source` `null` when no set applies, `direct` when it is the directory's own.
Unlike step 4 it includes the directory itself: the question is where a new pattern
would land, not what filtered an entry. The GUI's copy-on-write prompt is built on it.

Both are answered from [[The watch rule index]]: no store read per path.
